Z||000019||DreXteR||10-17-2001||11:03 AM||||Shawn41178 - Please, provide me more info regarding this situation. Why I should have to adjust the lifters? <P>I cant see any email in the Fierosound page.<P>Thanx<P>DreX||||reg||1
Z||000020||Neal||10-17-2001||12:24 PM||||The difference between fierosounds setup and what your proposing to do is that he used a FWD 3400 block, which is a roller block and has a guide plate at the top of the lifter valley so the roller lifters wont rotate, this guide plate also required that the aluminum piece at the bottom of the lower intake be "massaged".  Also to use the roller lifters he also had to use custom length pushrods.  These are the OEM hydrolic lifters.<P>The crane roller lifters you showed will NOT clear the lower intake at all.  Yes they are advertised to work but NOT with a fiero intake.  Also they look like solid lifters, which requre constant adjustments to work properly.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Shaun41178||10-17-2001||04:04 PM||||Drex I don't know if you would have to constatnly adjust the lifters or not.  It depends on if the lifters are hydraulic or if they are solid.  If they are hydraulic then they will only need to be adjusted once when you assemble it and that should be it.  With solid you have to keep adjusting them often as the clearences change.<P>If you don't know whether or not the lifters you have are solid or not then I would give Crane a call and find out.<P>And when I say adjust I mean you would need to take off the intake and the valve covers and adjust the rocker arm ball to make sure the valve lash is correct.  Lots of work if you ask me and lots of money in gaskets too.<P>The block you would need would need to be a Gen 3 style block.  Meaning one that came with a roller cam already.  I would search for a 3.1 block from a grand prix or a grand am from 95 to 01.  Those years has the roller engine.  Another thing to look for is if it says 3100 or 3400 on the top of the intake plenum then it has a roller cam which means it is setup to handle roller lifters already.<P>Also I would talk to that other guy that was referred to that used the 3400 block with a roller cam and fiero intake and see what all he had to do to get it to work.  Using a block and such that already came with a roller cam would be the easiest way to go however it can still be done with a block that didn't come from the factory with a roller cam.  I know a guy that used a 2.8 Fiero style block with a roller cam and then used a 3.4 camaro intake as the camaro intake is slightly different and has a little more clearence for a roller cam.  The reason I think for this is cause the 3.4 camaro lower intake does not have a provision for a cold start injector.  I could be wrong on that however.  <P>Let me know if you have anymore questions.||||reg||1
Z||000022||DreXteR||10-17-2001||09:56 PM||||Shawn41178 - The lifters are solid. <A HREF="http://members.tripod.com/TurboZ24/Specs.htm" TARGET=_blank>This guy</A> did it, that is why I asking all this. Dont know if he need to adjust his rockers or not. That block isnt the roller one, also he is using the 3.4 intake. <P>O man  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> now my project have another BIG stop. Find a 3100 block wouldnt be cheaper. Another delay....  has been a year already.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>I guess this is the Fiero Sh!et.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>For the brigth side, when I finish it will be all new and better than the day it came from the dealer.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  . . . . .  Some day  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P>The Sad DreX <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
